{
  "$comment": "#EXP-1815 (owner 2026-11-13, plan approved as drawn): WHERE A NOTIFICATION SHOWS, WHEN IT CLEARS, WHO MUTES IT ×4. Model = src/notification-delivery.ts; mirrors: desktop `domain::notification_delivery`, iOS ExpPush `NotificationDelivery.swift` (the Notification Service Extension links it), Android `domain/NotificationDelivery.kt`; every mirror replays every `*Cases` array. THREAD (`notificationThread`, `threadCases`): every notification belongs to ONE thread, the key it stacks and clears under: an issue row = `issue:<issueId>` (the issue wins, like inbox-route.json), a row naming a session = `session:<rootId>` (the CHAT, i.e. the session's root; a client that has not synced the session uses the session id), an `agent_signed_out` row = `device:<deviceId>`, anything else = `inbox`. The server puts it on every push as `dataKeys.thread`; iOS sets it as the content's `threadIdentifier` (the Notification Service Extension), Android as the notification's group AND tag (id = the notification id's hash), so one chat's notifications stack as one. A screen's own thread (`viewing`): an issue's page = `issue:<id>`, a chat or session page = `session:<rootId>`, a device's settings = `device:<id>`, the Inbox = `inbox`, which covers EVERY thread (it lists them all); any other screen = null. PRESENTATION (`notificationPresentation`, `presentCases`): `muted` (the source switches below) = `none` everywhere. The app ACTIVE (web: the page; desktop: a focused window; iOS/Android: foreground) and `viewing` equal to the thread or `inbox` = `none`: the content and the badge update, nothing pops. Active elsewhere = `toast`: THE toast (toast-stack.json), kind `info`, title = the row's title, description = its body, action `copy.toastAction` that opens the row like an inbox tap (inbox-route.json). Not active = `system`: desktop raises its OS notification, iOS and Android let the push show; web has no system channel, so it is `toast` there too (the toast waits behind the hidden tab). iOS: `willPresent` returns no presentation options while the app is in the foreground and the app shows the toast; Android: FcmService posts nothing to the NotificationManager while the app is in the foreground and hands the push to the app's toast. CLEARING (`dismissPayload`, `dismissCases`, `dismissMatches`, `matchCases`): reading a notification row on ANY device removes its system notification on EVERY device. The server marks rows read on `notifications.markRead` (the row), `markReadByIssue` (the issue's rows), `markAllRead` (all, or one team's), and NOW also on `sessions.markRead` (opening a chat or a session: that session's rows, plus the whole tree's when it is the root) and `sessions.setRead` read=true (each chat's tree); the rows that turned read in that write (never already-read ones) go out as ONE silent push per device of the reader: `{type: dismissType, ids}` (comma-joined notification ids, at most `limits.maxIds`), over that `{type, threads}` (distinct thread keys, at most `limits.maxThreads`), over that, or a mark-all, `{type, all: 1}` plus `teamId` when the write was one team's. Nothing turned read = no push. The silent push is the relay's `POST /push/silent` (APNs `apns-push-type: background`, `content-available: 1`, priority 5; FCM data-only, priority normal), sealed like every push (push-seal.json). A device removes each DELIVERED notification the payload matches: its `notificationId` in `ids`, its thread in `threads`, `all` with no `teamId`, or `all` with its `teamId` equal. iOS: the app's background-push handler (`application(_:didReceiveRemoteNotification:)`) opens it and removes the matching delivered requests (`removeDeliveredNotifications(withIdentifiers:)`); Android: FcmService cancels each matching active notification by tag and id; desktop gets no push and needs none: it removes its own OS notification when the synced row's `read_at` lands (same match by id). A dismiss push NEVER shows anything. SOURCE SWITCHES (`notificationSource`, `sourceCases`, `sourceAllowed`, `allowCases`): Settings › Notifications on all four platforms carries the member's switches, stored server-side in `user_notification_prefs.source_prefs` (`{assistant?, temporary?, agents?: {<agentId>: bool}}`, absent = on) and read/written with `notifications.emailPrefs` / `updateEmailPrefs` (`sourcePrefs`). A notification row naming a session has a SOURCE from that session's ROOT: its agent (`agentId`), else the agent that started it (`startedByAgentId`); an agent whose role is `assistant` = `assistant` (every one of the member's Assistants, one per team), any other agent (`agent`, `coordinator`) = `agent:<agentId>`, no agent = `temporary` (the composer's Temporary session). A row without a session has no source (null) and no switch applies (issue rows keep the per-type switches). The SERVER applies the switch when it writes the row, before push, desktop and email: a muted recipient still gets the inbox row, but with `pushed_at` NULL (and `emailed_at` stamped, so the digest never mails it) and no push; desktop and web raise nothing for a row whose `pushed_at` is NULL. SETTINGS (`settingsRows`, `settingsCases`): one group under the band `copy.sessionsBand`: `copy.assistant` then `copy.temporary`; then, when the member's teams have any team agents, a group under the band `copy.agentsBand`: one switch per agent (role `agent` or `coordinator`, never `assistant`), ordered by team name then agent name, label = the agent's name, detail = its team's name only when the member is in more than one team. The rows are the existing glass toggle rows inside glass groups under the section header (GlassSectionHeader / GlassGroup / GlassToggleRow and their native twins); no new component. They sit ABOVE the email card. ANDROID CHANNELS (`androidChannel`, `channelCases`): a push's `dataKeys.source` (+ `dataKeys.sourceName`, the agent's name) picks its channel: `assistant` = `channels.assistant`, `temporary` = `channels.temporary`, `agent:<id>` = `channels.agentPrefix` + id named after the agent inside the channel group `channels.agentsGroup`, no source = the app's existing issue channel (`channels.fallback`). The app creates a channel on first use; the system's channel settings keep sound and importance.",
